# [GENAREA](../wg/genarea.html)

## Summary

The GENAREA session at IETF 127 was a special topics session dedicated to discussing the impact, measurement, workflow integration, and potential policy interventions surrounding Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the IETF standards process. Co-chaired by Roman Danyliw and Mahesh Jethanandani, the session aimed to level-set community understanding and showcase data on AI activity across IETF mailing lists and Internet-Draft submissions. 

The session featured presentations on mailing list AI detection, Internet-Draft submission trends, automated meeting minute generation, and author disclosure considerations. During the discussion on AI content disclosure, a ceiling water leak forced an immediate evacuation of the meeting room, ending the physical session prematurely. No formal consensus calls, decisions, or dispatch actions were taken during the meeting.

## Key Discussion Points

### Chairs' Introduction
* Roman Danyliw and Mahesh Jethanandani opened the session and framed the discussion around situational awareness, level-setting, and brainstorming regarding AI's role in the standards process.
* Roman Danyliw noted that the Note Well applies and reiterated that the session would not include dispatching functions or consensus calls.
* Slide Deck: [Chairs' Introduction](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-genarea-chairs-introduction-00)

### Measurement Observations: Mailing List Traffic
* Jay Daley presented an empirical analysis of AI-generated text on IETF mailing lists using Pangram (an overweight LLM-based detection tool) analyzing messages from January 2025 onwards.
* Slide Deck: [AI generated text on IETF mailing lists](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-genarea-ai-generated-text-on-ietf-mailing-lists-00)
* Jay explained that an email reply extraction library was developed to separate new content from quoted text and signatures. The results showed a tenfold increase in AI-generated email traffic, peaking in August 2025 at approximately 27% of messages across analyzed lists.
* The analysis indicated that AI content was concentrated in a small subset of working groups (e.g., SKITT, MSC, agent-to-agent) and driven primarily by new senders/agent pseudonyms.
* John Guida (SKITT co-chair) noted that the influx of high-percentage AI threads in SKITT was driven by agent pseudonyms associated with a small number of individuals and was only halted when the chairs locked the mailing list.
* Pete Resnick asked whether the tool distinguishes between AI translation and AI generation. Jay Daley responded that AI-translated text yields lower confidence scores than fully AI-generated text.

### Measurement Observations: Internet-Draft Submissions
* Robert Sparks presented submission statistics for Internet-Drafts (I-Ds) over the period 2016–2026, focusing on -00 drafts.
* Slide Deck: [Internet Draft Submissions, 2016-2026](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-genarea-internet-draft-submissions-2016-2026-00)
* In 2026, initial (-00) draft submissions nearly doubled. Half of the authors submitting -00 drafts in 2026 were first-time contributors. 
* Robert Sparks emphasized that regardless of the underlying cause or AI classification, the sheer volume increase creates a operational "fire hose" challenge for human review and community processing.
* Paul Hoffman noted that AI tools increase output for legitimate human authors as well as generating low-quality submissions, increasing overall community workload.
* Farzaneh Badiei shared that AI tools assisted them as a newcomer in navigating the technical submission process. Stuart Cheshire welcomed new contributors and cautioned against treating measurement data as an effort to discourage newcomers.

### AI Workflows: Automated Minutes
* Eric Rescorla (ekr) presented `auto-minutes`, an automated tool that ingests meeting audio/video, transcribes it via Deepgram (with speaker diarization), cleans and summarizes the transcript via Gemini, and generates Markdown meeting minutes.
* Slide Deck: [Auto Minutes](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-genarea-auto-minutes-00)
* The tool runs via GitHub Actions at an estimated operational cost of $100–$200 per IETF meeting. 
* Eric Rescorla identified key integration opportunities for Datatracker, such as API access for chair authentication and direct minute uploads, as well as accessing Meetecho mic queue state to improve speaker identification.
* Eric Rescorla emphasized that we need conventions around how to work with this kind of material while understanding that no human was standing behind the contents.
* David Schinazi and Leslie Daigle spoke in favor of using automated minutes as a helpful baseline for working group chairs.
* Yixin Sun highlighted research on using AI tools to assist human reviewers in catching errata and formatting errors in drafts prior to RFC publication.
* Jay Daley raised potential PII/privacy considerations regarding voice recognition. Eric Rescorla clarified that the tool relies on in-session audio diarization and self-identified names in the transcript rather than building cross-session voiceprint databases.

### Interventions & Policy: AI Content Disclosure
* Brian Carpenter introduced the topic of participant responsibility and questioned whether the IETF should expect authors to disclose when substantive content in drafts, emails, or RFCs is generated by AI.
* Slide Deck: [Disclosure, or An AI did my homework](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-genarea-disclosure-or-an-ai-did-my-homework-00)
* Phillip Hallam-Baker supported requiring disclosure to inform readers of how content was produced.
* Bron Gondwana, Martin Thomson, and Eric Rescorla spoke against mandatory disclosure, arguing that human submitters must remain 100% accountable for all submitted content regardless of the tools used. They also noted that distinguishing between AI text generation and advanced grammar/editing checkers is practically unenforceable.

### Room Evacuation
* During the discussion of Brian Carpenter's presentation, heavy rainfall caused a roof leak directly onto the stage area. Co-chair Roman Danyliw instructed all in-person participants to immediately evacuate the room for safety, ending the physical session.

## Decisions and Action Items

* No formal decisions or action items were established during the session.

